      <section class="assessment-section">
        <h2>Terms And Abbreviations</h2>
        <p class="assessment-context">Reference Appendix</p>
        <p>Definitions for report-specific codes, scales, protocol language, and measurement shorthand.</p>
<details class="assessment-reference disclosure-group domain-disclosure"><summary><span class="disclosure-label">Terms And Abbreviations<span class="disclosure-outcomes">Terms: 14</span></span><span class="disclosure-chevron" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><div class="assessment-section__content">
        <dl class="assessment-facts">
          <div><dt class="section-abstract__label">Terms</dt><dd>14</dd></div>
        </dl>

        <dl class="reader-key-grid">
          <div class="reader-key-item">
            <dt>MCP</dt>
            <dd>Model Context Protocol, the communication protocol evaluated by this report.</dd>
          </div>
          <div class="reader-key-item">
            <dt>MCP specification profile</dt>
            <dd>The dated MCP rules and schema version selected for validation.</dd>
          </div>
          <div class="reader-key-item">
            <dt>Priority 1-4</dt>
            <dd>Remediation dependency order: Priority 1 should be addressed first and Priority 4 later. Priority is not severity.</dd>
          </div>
          <div class="reader-key-item">
            <dt>Certificate eligibility</dt>
            <dd>A deterministic decision that states whether every requirement of the selected assurance profile is satisfied.</dd>
          </div>
          <div class="reader-key-item">
            <dt>Typed claim</dt>
            <dd>A conclusion bound to authority, requirement level, applicability, confidence, and immutable evidence references.</dd>
          </div>
          <div class="reader-key-item">
            <dt>Independent assessments</dt>
            <dd>Core conformance, security assurance, and operational readiness are reduced separately and never averaged into one score.</dd>
          </div>
          <div class="reader-key-item">
            <dt>Authority labels</dt>
            <dd>Specification means a protocol requirement; Guideline means recommended practice; Heuristic means deterministic advisory analysis; Operational means runtime or infrastructure evidence.</dd>
          </div>
          <div class="reader-key-item">
            <dt>JSON-RPC / HTTP</dt>
            <dd>JSON-RPC is JavaScript Object Notation (JSON) for remote procedure call (RPC) messages used by MCP. HTTP is the Hypertext Transfer Protocol used to transport messages in this run.</dd>
          </div>
          <div class="reader-key-item">
            <dt>RFC 2119: MUST / SHOULD / MAY</dt>
            <dd>Requirement strength: MUST is required, SHOULD is recommended unless a justified exception exists, and MAY is optional.</dd>
          </div>
          <div class="reader-key-item">
            <dt>P50 / P95 / P99</dt>
            <dd>Latency percentiles: 50%, 95%, or 99% of measured requests completed at or below the reported value.</dd>
          </div>
          <div class="reader-key-item">
            <dt>Units and time</dt>
            <dd>ms means milliseconds, req/s means requests per second, and UTC means Coordinated Universal Time.</dd>
          </div>
          <div class="reader-key-item">
            <dt>AI / LLM / CLI / UX</dt>
            <dd>Artificial intelligence; large language model; command-line interface; and user experience.</dd>
          </div>
          <div class="reader-key-item">
            <dt>VS Code</dt>
            <dd>Visual Studio Code, a supported client profile named in compatibility results.</dd>
          </div>
          <div class="reader-key-item">
            <dt>Identifiers: ID / UUID</dt>
            <dd>ID means identifier. UUID means Universally Unique Identifier. Validation and rule identifiers are stable machine references, not scores.</dd>
          </div>
        </dl>
        </div></details></section>

